</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<p data-start=3D"4789" data-end=3D"4926" class=3D"">Cela r=C3=A9cup=C3=A9re=
ra les modifications de la branche <code data-start=3D"4837" data-end=3D"48=
48">feature-x</code> du d=C3=A9p=C3=B4t distant <code data-start=3D"4866" d=
ata-end=3D"4874">origin</code> et les fusionnera avec votre branche locale =
active.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"4928" data-end=3D"4931" class=3D"">
<h3 data-start=3D"4933" data-end=3D"4980" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
4937" data-end=3D"4980">5.2. Utilisation de <code data-start=3D"4959" data-=
end=3D"4978">git pull --rebase</code></strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"4982" data-end=3D"5260" class=3D"">Par d=C3=A9faut, <code =
data-start=3D"4994" data-end=3D"5004">git pull</code> effectue une fusion d=
es modifications distantes. Cependant, parfois vous pr=C3=A9f=C3=A9rez que =
vos commits locaux soient appliqu=C3=A9s apr=C3=A8s les commits distants, c=
e qui est r=C3=A9alis=C3=A9 par une <strong data-start=3D"5182" data-end=3D=
"5192">rebase</strong>. Cela permet d'avoir un historique lin=C3=A9aire san=
s commits de fusion.</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"5262" data-end=3D"5281" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
5266" data-end=3D"5281">Commandes :</strong></h3>
